<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · War and march · 1861 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Battle Hymn of the Republic</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A hymn Julia Ward Howe wrote in one night in 1861, set to a soldiers' marching tune, for the side fighting against slavery.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Julia Ward Howe</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (the camp-meeting tune of &quot;Say, Brothers will you Meet Us&quot;, also sung as &quot;John Brown's Body&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Union supporters and abolitionists in the American Civil War; later Americans generally</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Willard Hotel, Washington, D.C., United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>written the night of 18 November 1861; first published February 1862 in The Atlantic Monthly</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Julia Ward Howe (published 1862; she died in 1910))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The tune came from a folk hymn of the revivalist camp meetings of the late 1700s, &quot;Say, Brothers will you Meet Us&quot;, first published in the early 1800s; its first known printed form was &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>'s Happy Shore&quot;. By the 1850s it carried the &quot;Glory, glory, hallelujah&quot; chorus, and the tune spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In the first months of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, soldiers of the 2nd Infantry Battalion of the Massachusetts militia, the &quot;Tiger&quot; Battalion, made up new words to it. According to George Kimball's account of 1890, they began by teasing a Scottish comrade named John Brown, who shared his name with the abolitionist executed after the raid on Harpers Ferry, and the jokes grew into verses about Brown's body lying in the grave while his soul marched on. &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a>&quot; was publicly played, perhaps for the first time, at a flag-raising at Fort Warren near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> on 12 May 1861, and the battalion soon prepared the words for publication with the publisher C. S. Hall.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julia-ward-howe">Julia Ward Howe</a>, an abolitionist writer, heard troops singing the song at a review outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, on Munson's Hill near Bailey's Crossroads, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. Her husband, Samuel Gridley Howe, was an anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> leader and one of the Secret Six who had funded John Brown. Her companion at the review, the Reverend James Freeman Clarke, suggested that she write new words for the soldiers' song. Staying at the Willard Hotel, she woke before dawn on 19 November 1861 with the lines forming in her mind and wrote them down in the half-dark with an old pencil stub. She sold the poem to The Atlantic Monthly for $4, and it appeared on the front page of the February 1862 issue; a sixth verse in her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was not printed. Where the soldiers' words were rough and comic, Howe's tied the Union cause to God's judgment, drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> 63 and the Book of Revelation. In 1863 the Supervisory Committee for Recruiting Colored Regiments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> printed it as a broadside.</p><p>After the war it became one of the most used American patriotic songs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marian-anderson">Marian Anderson</a> sang it in 1953 to a television audience of 60 million. The Mormon Tabernacle Choir's recording reached number 13 on the Billboard Hot 100 and won a Grammy in 1960; the choir sang it on national television on 22 November 1963, the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> was killed, and Judy Garland sang it on her show that December when CBS would not let her dedicate a programme to him. Andy Williams recorded it at Robert Kennedy's funeral in 1968, and it was sung at the burial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyndon-b-johnson">Lyndon B. Johnson</a> in 1973 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-carter">Jimmy Carter</a>'s funeral ceremony in 2025.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> used its words in his speeches, among them &quot;How Long, Not Long&quot; at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a> on 25 March 1965, at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selma-to-montgomery-marches">Selma to Montgomery march</a>, and his last sermon in Memphis on 3 April 1968, the night before he was assassinated. The tune also carried many other words: the Civil War &quot;Marching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a>&quot; of Black soldiers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-twain">Mark Twain</a>'s satire on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a> in 1901, the labour song &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> Forever&quot;, college fight songs, children's parodies, and football chants that began at Tottenham Hotspur in 1961 and spread through British and Australian sport. It has also been turned to hateful ends, as in the racist &quot;The White Man <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marches</a> On&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of apocalyptic faith: the singer describes the coming of God's judgment as if seen among the camps and armies of the war, and calls on the listener to answer it. It joins the Union's fight to a religious duty to end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, with a solemn, marching tone that rises to the repeated &quot;Glory, hallelujah&quot; chorus.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Mine eyes have seen the glory of the coming of the Lord:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He is trampling out the vintage where the grapes of wrath are stored;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He hath loosed the fateful lightning of His terrible swift sword:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>His truth is marching on.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I have seen Him in the watch-fires of a hundred circling camps,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>They have builded Him an altar in the evening dews and damps;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I can read His righteous sentence by the dim and flaring lamps:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>His day is marching on.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I have read a fiery gospel writ in burnished rows of steel:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;As ye deal with my contemners, so with you My grace shall deal;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Let the Hero, born of woman, crush the serpent with his heel,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Since God is marching on&quot;.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He has sounded forth the trumpet that shall never call retreat;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He is sifting out the hearts of men before His judgment-seat:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh, be swift, my soul, to answer Him! be jubilant, my feet!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our God is marching on.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In the beauty of the lilies Christ was born across the sea,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With a glory in his bosom that transfigures you and me:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As He died to make men holy, let us die to make men free,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>While God is marching on.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Julia Ward Howe, The Atlantic Monthly (February 1862). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Atlantic_Monthly%2FBattle_Hymn_of_the_Republic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="G9anfFdAr3E" data-title="Battle Hymn of the Republic: The Tabernacle Choir, live from West Point"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">The Tabernacle Choir, live from West Point</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Tabernacle Choir at Temple Square. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G9anfFdAr3E" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Grapes of Wrath (John Steinbeck)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1939</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title taken from the first verse</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">In the Beauty of the Lilies (John Updike)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title taken from the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Terrible Swift Sword; Never Call Retreat (Bruce Catton)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">titles taken from the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Marching Song of the First Arkansas</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">Civil War era</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Battle Hymn of the Republic, Updated (Mark Twain)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1901</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Solidarity Forever</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">20th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Glory Glory to Old Georgia</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1909</span><span class="sg-chain-h">college rally song, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Glory, glory, hallelujah (Tottenham Hotspur supporters)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1961</span><span class="sg-chain-h">football chant</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">How Long, Not Long (Martin Luther King Jr.)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1965</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">These Things Take Time (The Smiths)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1983</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">An American Trilogy (Mickey Newbury)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1972</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody used in medley</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">In the Name of God (Dream Theater)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2003</span><span class="sg-chain-h">words used</span></li><li><a href="/songs/glory-to-hong-kong">Glory to Hong Kong</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">2019</span><span class="sg-chain-h">model named by the composer</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Say, Brothers will you Meet Us (Canaan's Happy Shore)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">late 1700s; printed early 1800s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the tune and chorus</span></li><li><a href="/songs/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1861</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the soldiers' song whose tune Howe wrote new words for</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Isaiah 63:1–6; Revelation 14:14–19; Revelation 19</span><span class="sg-chain-h">biblical passages alluded to</span></li></ul></section>
